1: # P3K.38. Intrinsic minimal scalar winding for the complete fixed-source catalogue
2: 
3: Status: complete proof and exact certificate, submitted for independent
4: audit. No automorphism mapping class is inferred here.
5: 
6: ## 1. Statement and the actual parameter being tested
7: 
8: Fix any of the thirty nonempty branches of P3F.53, including its actual
9: source coefficients, vertical line divisor, logarithmic norms, affine
10: cap maps, finite line characters, order-one clutches, and allowed
11: index-one fan. We write its line and source translation as
12: \[
13:  \mathcal M=\mathcal O(aG-O+D),\qquad Q=-bG,
14:  \qquad ab=d,
15: \]
16: with the notation and actual divisor comparisons of P3F.51--53. Let
17: \(m_i>1\) be its effective cap orders and put
18: \(M=\prod_i m_i\); an absent or order-one slot contributes one.
19: All norms \(n_i\) and the ordinary framing term are retained in
20: P3K.32's signed Seifert equation. In particular,
21: \(\gcd(m_i,n_i)=1\), and the nontrivial \(m_i\) are pairwise
22: coprime.
23: 
24: The actual scalar is the multiplier in
25: \[
26:                          B_\lambda=\lambda B_0.       \tag{IM.1}
27: \]
28: We continue the fixed construction on the logarithm cover of a small
29: punctured \(\lambda\)-disc. Its data at two logarithm values differing
30: by \(2\pi iN\) have a biholomorphic endpoint identification exactly
31: when
32: \[
33:                               N\in (M/b)\mathbb Z.     \tag{IM.2}
34: \]
35: This assertion allows every biholomorphic map between the resulting
36: threefolds. It imposes no preservation of a chosen elliptic marking,
37: line-circle action, source section, or toric chart on that map.
38: The positive integer \(M/b\) is therefore the least positive scalar
39: winding. Sufficiency is the actual family of P3K.35,
40: \[
41:                      \lambda=w^{M/b},\qquad w=r^b,
42:                      \lambda=r^M.                     \tag{IM.3}
43: \]
44: We prove necessity by intrinsic invariants of its multiple fibres.
45: 
46: For the component-supported OS49 family of P3K.34 and P3K.37, with
47: \(m=3k\), logarithmic vector
48: \(n(e_\gamma-e_u)+d_\delta\delta\), and \(n=\pm1\), the
49: corresponding least positive scalar winding is \(k\). Its actual
50: closed parameter is \(\lambda=w^k\), \(w=r^3\).
51: The argument for that statement uses its actual central Picard point
52: \(G'_0\); it uses no whole-fibre height formula.
53: 
54: ## 2. Every endpoint map preserves each nontrivial multiple fibre
55: 
56: We first make the fibration intrinsic. Every threefold in question is
57: diffeomorphic to \(S^6\), by the integral topology of P3F.53 and
58: P3K.32, with the actual fan changes of P3F.35. Thus
59: \(H^2(Y;\mathbb R)=0\). A general fibre \(F\) of the displayed
60: map \(f:Y\to\mathbb P^1\) is a compact complex torus, hence is
61: Kähler. A horizontal effective divisor \(Z\) on \(Y\) would
62: restrict to a nonzero effective divisor \(Z|_F\) with zero real
63: first Chern class. For a Kähler form \(\omega\) on \(F\),
64: \[
65:  0=\int_F c_1(\mathcal O(Z)|_F)\wedge\omega
66:    =\int_{Z|_F}\omega>0,
67: \]
68: a contradiction. Every effective divisor is consequently vertical.
69: 
70: Let \(g\) be a meromorphic function on \(Y\). Its polar divisor
71: is vertical, so on a general compact torus fibre its restriction
72: has no polar divisor. Hartogs extension across its possible
73: codimension-two indeterminacy set makes that restriction holomorphic,
74: and hence constant. To see the descent at each base value, choose a
75: local coordinate \(q\). A sufficiently large power of \(q\circ f\)
76: cancels all divisorial poles on a small proper inverse image. The
77: resulting function is holomorphic by Hartogs. The fibres are connected;
78: the Stein factorization of this proper map to a normal disc therefore
79: gives \(f_*\mathcal O_Y=\mathcal O\). The holomorphic function
80: descends, and division by the same power of \(q\) gives a meromorphic
81: function on the disc. We have proved the literal field equality
82: \[
83:                     \mathbb C(Y)=f^*\mathbb C(t).       \tag{IM.4}
84: \]
85: 
86: A biholomorphism between endpoint threefolds induces an automorphism
87: of this field and hence a Möbius transformation of the base. The
88: equality of meromorphic maps extends over the entire threefold.
89: Divisorial multiplicities of its fibres are preserved. In the actual
90: cap chart the base function is \(q=z^{m_i}\), the root central
91: fibre is reduced, and the quotient is free with transitive component
92: action; its fibre is exactly \(m_i S_i\). The ordinary models
93: have divisorial gcd one. At the toric filling the same fact is the
94: primitivity of its physical valuation vector: the component orders
95: have gcd one, and changing the index-one fan retains that vector.
96: Thus the displayed effective caps are precisely the fibres of
97: multiplicity greater than one.
98: 
99: There are at most two such caps. Their coprime orders are distinct.
100: Every endpoint biholomorphism consequently carries each \(S_i\)
101: to the continuation of the same \(S_i\). This conclusion allows
102: permutations of ordinary fibres, including ones with the same Kodaira
103: type, and arbitrary source automorphisms. A biholomorphism of the
104: reduced supports lifts uniquely to their normalizations, by the
105: universal property of normalization. We may therefore use the
106: Albanese curve of a smooth cap support, or of its normalization,
107: without requiring any additional equivariance of the endpoint map.
108: 
109: ## 3. The exact period lattice at a good-reduction cap
110: 
111: Fix one good cap, and write its order and norm as \(m,n\).
112: The actual root line has central restriction
113: \[
114:                           N_0=\mathcal O_E(aG'_0-O).
115:                                                                \tag{IM.5}
116: \]
117: This is the line supplied by the root/quotient divisor diagrams in
118: P3F.41--42 and P3F.46--47. For every compatible vertical divisor
119: and fixed clutch, P3F.52's direct-image evaluation compares it with
120: (IM.5) by an actual base line and a fixed deck character. A base
121: line has trivial restriction to \(E\), and a character changes
122: the lift, not the underlying central line. Let
123: \[
124:                          t=\operatorname{ord}(aG'_0).
125:                                                                \tag{IM.6}
126: \]
127: This order includes the value one for the zero Picard point.
128: 
129: The literal minimal-root substitutions of P3F.51 give the following
130: points and automorphisms. At a good III or III* root, \(G'_0\)
131: is a nonzero point of order two and the deck derivative has order
132: four. At a good IV or IV* root it is a nonzero point of order three
133: and the derivative has order three. At a good II root the point
134: is \(O\), and the derivative has order six. In particular, with
135: \(o=\operatorname{ord}(G'_0)\),
136: \[
137:   t=\frac{o}{\gcd(o,a)},\qquad
138:   o=2,3,1\quad\hbox{in those three cases}.             \tag{IM.7}
139: \]
140: The program retains this multiplication by \(a\). For example the
141: good E7 line with \(a=2\) has order one, whereas that with \(a=1\)
142: has order two.
143: 
144: Write the central source cap map as \(z\mapsto Dz+R\) on
145: \(E=\mathbb C/\Lambda_E\). Here \(D\ne1\), its order divides
146: \(m\), and \(1+D+\cdots+D^{m-1}=0\). The source part of
147: \(A^m=B^n\) gives \(nbG'_0=0\). We also have \(mG'_0=0\).
148: Since \(\gcd(m,n)=1\), the Bezout identity gives \(bG'_0=0\).
149: The actual \(B_0\) therefore covers the identity on \(E\), and
150: acts on \(N_0\) by a single nonzero constant \(c\). Put
151: \[
152:                   \tau=\frac{\log(c\lambda)}{2\pi i}.
153:                                                                \tag{IM.8}
154: \]
155: 
156: We now exhibit every period rather than just an invariant subspace.
157: The torsion line (IM.5) has its finite unitary character
158: \(\chi:\Lambda_E\to\mu_t\), of exact image order \(t\).
159: In a logarithmic line coordinate \(\eta\), its universal cover is
160: \(\mathbb C^2\), with generators
161: \[
162:  (z,\eta)\longmapsto(z+\omega,\eta+\theta_\omega),
163:  \quad e^{2\pi i\theta_\omega}=\chi(\omega),
164:  \qquad (z,\eta)\longmapsto(z,\eta+1).
165:                                                                \tag{IM.9}
166: \]
167: Here every \(\theta_\omega\) is real, and their classes together
168: with \(\mathbb Z\) generate exactly \((1/t)\mathbb Z\).
169: The quotient by \(B_\lambda\) adds the period \((0,\tau)\)
170: and is the actual compact central abelian surface.
171: 
172: The equality \(D^*N_0=N_0\) makes the finite unitary character
173: \(D\)-invariant. Thus an affine source map \(Dz+R\) has a
174: constant line lift in (IM.9). Every other holomorphic lift differs
175: by an invertible holomorphic function on the compact elliptic curve,
176: which is constant. The actual cap generator can consequently be
177: written, with its fixed character retained, as
178: \[
179:  A_\lambda(z,\eta)
180:        =(Dz+R,\eta+n\tau/m+\kappa).                  \tag{IM.10}
181: \]
182: The exact norm gives \(m\kappa\in\mathbb Z\): the source lift
183: has zero translation after \(m\) iterations because the geometric
184: sum of the powers of \(D\) is zero, and a deck period with zero
185: source coordinate has integral line coordinate. We retain \(\kappa\) in every
186: formula below; no finite character is discarded.
187: 
188: All holomorphic one-forms on the abelian cover are constant.
189: The affine linear part \(\operatorname{diag}(D,1)\) fixes just
190: the line generated by \(d\eta\). Integration over the full
191: affine deck group (IM.9)--(IM.10) gives its complete period image
192: \[
193:  \mathcal P=(1/t)\mathbb Z+\tau\mathbb Z
194:                          +(n\tau/m+\kappa)\mathbb Z. \tag{IM.11}
195: \]
196: Choose integers \(x,y\) with \(mx+ny=1\). Then the actual
197: period combination
198: \(x\tau+y(n\tau/m+\kappa)\) is
199: \(\beta=\tau/m+y\kappa\), and conversely
200: \[
201:   \tau=m\beta-my\kappa,\qquad
202:   n\tau/m+\kappa=n\beta+mx\kappa.
203:                                                                \tag{IM.12}
204: \]
205: The remainders belong to \((1/t)\mathbb Z\). These equations
206: prove the lattice equality, with its arrows in both directions,
207: \[
208:                  \mathcal P=(1/t)\mathbb Z
209:                              +(\tau/m+y\kappa)\mathbb Z.
210:                                                                \tag{IM.13}
211: \]
212: Replacing \((x,y)\) by \((x+ns,y-ms)\) changes the second
213: generator by the first lattice, so the result is independent of
214: the Bezout representative.
215: 
216: The map induced by \(\eta\) to \(\mathbb C/\mathcal P\)
217: is the Albanese map. This follows directly from the invariant
218: one-form and its complete integration lattice; equivalently its
219: fibre is the elliptic cover with period kernel \(\ker\chi\).
220: Multiplication by \(t\) gives its Tate coordinate
221: \[
222:            Q_i=e^{2\pi i t y\kappa}(c\lambda)^{t/m}.
223:                                                                \tag{IM.14}
224: \]
225: The norm \(n\), source affine term, and fixed line character occur
226: in the displayed constant. The exponent is determined by the actual
227: Picard point (IM.6).
228: 
229: ## 4. The normalization and its period at a reduced-root cap
230: 
231: We now use an actual reduced root of type II, III, IV, or I2. Its
232: central curve has, respectively, \(r=1,2,3,2\) rational components
233: in its normalization. We retain the precise root line, whose degree
234: on each component is zero by the line diagrams in P3F.36--49 and
235: P3F.52. Its pullback to each \(\mathbb P^1\) is therefore
236: trivial. This assertion does not trivialize the line on the singular
237: curve, nor discard its nodal or infinitesimal gluing data.
238: 
239: On the actual root line the two maps satisfy
240: \[
241:  B_\lambda=\lambda B_0,\qquad
242:  A_\lambda=\lambda^{n/m}A_0,\qquad
243:  A_\lambda^m=B_\lambda^n,
244:  \qquad[A_\lambda,B_\lambda]=1.                       \tag{IM.15}
245: \]
246: The fractional power here is on the chosen logarithm cover of the
247: scalar disc. For integers \(x,y\) with \(mx+ny=1\), form the
248: literal holomorphic line map
249: \[
250:                          H=B_\lambda^xA_\lambda^y.
251:                                                                \tag{IM.16}
252: \]
253: Negative powers are allowed because both maps are invertible in
254: this cap. Their exact norm and commutativity prove
255: \[
256:  H^m=B_\lambda^{mx+ny}=B_\lambda,\qquad
257:  H^n A_\lambda^{-1}
258:         =(B_\lambda^nA_\lambda^{-m})^x=1.
259:                                                                \tag{IM.17}
260: \]
261: Consequently their actual action group is precisely \(\langle H\rangle\).
262: 
263: The sphere cap's component action is transitive, as established by
264: the integral cap constructions. Since it is now generated by \(H\),
265: that generator cycles all \(r\) normalization components. Choose
266: fixed trivializations of their degree-zero lines and label the
267: components in this cyclic order. On them the literal maps have form
268: \[
269:  (z,V,j)\longmapsto
270:         (h_j(z),c_j\lambda^{1/m}V,j+1).               \tag{IM.18}
271: \]
272: The source maps \(h_j\) are Möbius transformations, independent
273: of \(\lambda\). The coefficients \(c_j\) are constant in
274: \(z\): an invertible map between the two trivial lines on compact
275: \(\mathbb P^1\) is a nowhere-zero holomorphic function on that
276: curve. The exponent is exactly
277: \(x+yn/m=1/m\). The constants retain every finite character and
278: the fixed singular-line gluing.
279: 
280: The stabilizer of one component is \(\langle H^r\rangle\).
281: Its quotient is therefore the explicit surface
282: \[
283:   \widetilde S_i=
284:   (\mathbb P^1\times\mathbb C^*)/langle
285:        (z,V)\longmapsto(h(z),c_i\lambda^{r/m}V)
286:                                       \rangle .       \tag{IM.19}
287: \]
288: This is the normalization of the reduced cap support. To check the
289: map, normalize the root central curve and pull back its line. The
290: resulting finite map is equivariant for (IM.16). For sufficiently
291: small \(|\lambda|\), \(H^r\) strictly contracts the line norm,
292: so its action is free and properly discontinuous. Quotienting the
293: finite equivariant map, using compact fundamental annuli, gives a
294: finite map from (IM.19) to the actual central support. It is
295: birational because it is an isomorphism over the smooth locus of
296: the root central curve. The source is normal, so the finite map is
297: its normalization. This establishes the quotient arrow, including
298: the cuspidal type II and the multiple-intersection type III/IV
299: central curves.
300: 
301: The actual projection \([(z,V)]\mapsto[V]\) makes (IM.19) a
302: holomorphic \(\mathbb P^1\)-bundle over
303: \[
304:             E_{Q_i}=\mathbb C^*/Q_i^{\mathbb Z},
305:             \qquad Q_i=c_i\lambda^{r/m}.             \tag{IM.20}
306: \]
307: All holomorphic one-forms come from this elliptic curve, because
308: they vanish along the \(\mathbb P^1\) fibres. The homotopy exact
309: sequence of the displayed locally trivial bundle gives an
310: isomorphism on fundamental groups. Thus its full integration
311: lattice, and its Albanese curve, are exactly those of (IM.20).
312: In particular the source Möbius monodromy \(h\) introduces no
313: unrecorded isogeny into the Albanese period.
314: 
315: For the quadratic I1* cap, \(r=m=2\). Here \(B\) preserves
316: the two components while \(A\) exchanges them; (IM.17) still
317: cycles them and gives \(Q_i=c_i\lambda\). This cap imposes
318: no scalar-cover factor on its own. The reduced III and IV root
319: progressions have \(\gcd(m,r)=1\); they retain the full factor
320: \(m\). The type II normalization has \(r=1\).
321: 
322: ## 5. Intrinsic obstruction to every smaller winding
323: 
324: For a small nonzero Tate coordinate we have
325: \(j(Q)=Q^{-1}+744+O(Q)\), with the same normalization as in
326: P3K.36. For any root of unity \(\zeta\ne1\),
327: \[
328:       Q\bigl(j(Q)-j(\zeta Q)\bigr)
329:                          \longrightarrow1-\zeta^{-1}\ne0.
330:                                                                \tag{IM.21}
331: \]
332: There are only finitely many phase ratios for each fixed cap and
333: finitely many caps. We shrink the scalar disc once so that all the
334: nontrivial differences in (IM.21) are nonzero there.
335: 
336: After scalar winding \(N\), formula (IM.14) multiplies \(Q_i\)
337: by \(e^{2\pi iNt_i/m_i}\) at a good cap, and formula (IM.20)
338: multiplies it by \(e^{2\pi iNr_i/m_i}\) at a reduced cap. An
339: endpoint biholomorphism would give an isomorphism of the intrinsic
340: Albanese curves in Section 2, so it would make their \(j\)-invariants
341: equal. Equations (IM.21) therefore force, respectively,
342: \[
343:  m_i\mid Nt_i,
344:  \qquad m_i\mid Nr_i.                                \tag{IM.22}
345: \]
346: Thus the exact local necessary denominators are
347: \[
348:  d_i=\frac{m_i}{\gcd(m_i,t_i)}\quad\hbox{for a good root},
349:  \qquad
350:  d_i=\frac{m_i}{\gcd(m_i,r_i)}\quad\hbox{for a reduced root}.
351:                                                                \tag{IM.23}
352: \]
353: Each \(d_i\) divides \(m_i\), so distinct \(d_i\) are coprime.
354: The combined necessary winding is their product.
355: 
356: The actual root cases have the following removed factors:
357: 
358: | Root type | Actual central datum | Factor \(m_i/d_i\) |
359: |---|---|---:|
360: | Good III or III* | \(t_i=2/\gcd(2,a)\) | \(t_i\) |
361: | Good IV or IV* | \(t_i=3/\gcd(3,a)\) | \(t_i\) |
362: | Good II | \(t_i=1\) | 1 |
363: | Reduced III, orders \(4j+1\) or \(4j+3\) | \(r_i=2\) | 1 |
364: | Reduced IV, orders \(3j+1\) or \(3j+2\) | \(r_i=3\) | 1 |
365: | Reduced II | \(r_i=1\) | 1 |
366: | Quadratic I1* root I2 | \(m_i=r_i=2\) | 2 |
367: | Absent or order-one slot | ordinary marked cap | 1 |
368: 
369: In every nonempty F53 branch the product of these factors is exactly
370: the specified \(b\). The complete branch list and the exact
371: residue-class verification are recorded below and in the certificate.
372: Consequently
373: \[
374:                        \prod_i d_i=M/b.                \tag{IM.24}
375: \]
376: This proves the necessity in (IM.2). The quotient constructed in
377: P3K.35 proves its sufficiency, including every retained order-one
378: clutch and all allowed fans. Pulling that actual family back by
379: \(w\mapsto w^q\), or reversing the continuation direction,
380: gives the endpoint identifications for every integer multiple.
381: No statement about the mapping class of an additional endpoint
382: automorphism is required or deduced.
383: 
384: ## 6. The component-supported OS49 cap
385: 
386: The good IV* root of P3F.26 has the actual central line
387: \(\mathcal O(G'_0-O)\), where \(G'_0\) is a nonzero
388: three-torsion point. Its order is \(m=3k\). The component-supported
389: line at the I2 fibre changes neither this Picard point nor the
390: central IV* root diagram. The cap calculation of Section 3 therefore
391: has \(t=3\). Its intrinsic Albanese Tate parameter has exponent
392: \(3/m=1/k\).
393: 
394: For completeness we retain the mixed logarithmic marking directly.
395: With the actual covector marking of P3F.26, invariant covectors are
396: \(\psi\) and \(h=-w+3\delta\). In the coherent central period
397: matrix
398: \[
399:  \Pi=[(-3\mu,\beta),(-\tau_E,-\mu),(1,0),(0,1)],
400:  \qquad \tau_E^2-3\tau_E+3=0,\quad \mu=\tau_E/3,
401: \]
402: the invariant holomorphic form is \(-da+3db\). Its period row
403: is \(h+T\psi\), where
404: \[
405:  T=3\beta+\tau_E,
406:  \qquad \beta=\frac{\log\lambda}{2\pi i}+c_0.
407: \]
408: The actual affine vector
409: \(n(e_\gamma-e_u)+d_\delta\delta\) adds
410: \((nT+3d_\delta)/m\). For \(n=\pm1\), the complete period
411: lattice is
412: \[
413:  \mathbb Z+\mathbb Z\frac{T+3nd_\delta}{m}.
414:                                                                \tag{IM.25}
415: \]
416: Indeed the added period is \(n\) times the displayed second
417: generator, and \(T\) differs from \(m\) times that generator
418: by the integer \(3nd_\delta\). The generator \(h\) gives the
419: primitive real period one. This proves directly that the fixed
420: delta term and either norm sign affect the constant, while
421: \[
422:                         Q_i=c_i\lambda^{1/k}.          \tag{IM.26}
423: \]
424: The algebraic-reduction argument of Section 2 applies to these
425: actual spheres as well. Their unique nontrivial multiple fibre has
426: order \(3k\), so every endpoint map preserves it. Equation (IM.21)
427: forces \(k\mid N\). P3K.37 supplies the exact quotient
428: \(\lambda=w^k\), proving sharpness. This conclusion uses the
429: actual cap periods; the component line square remains the separate
430: value \(-2k\) established in P3K.34.
431: 
432: ## 7. Executed certificate and complete branch coverage
433: 
434: The program \path{code/part3/scalar_cap_minimality.py} verifies the
435: two-way good-cap period basis, the exact cyclic-generator identities,
436: the multiplication of the actual point by \(a\), and all gcd
437: formulas on every residue class modulo the finite central order.
438: It checks all thirty nonempty branches against \(M/b\), and
439: retains the impossible good III*/good II branch as excluded by its
440: common even cap orders. It does not replace a geometric period map
441: by a numerical inference. Its 163 exact checks pass; full output is
442: in the matching JSON and OUT files.
443: 
444: The parameters in the following table retain all positivity bounds,
445: coprimality conditions, norm equations, and finite characters in the
446: corresponding F53 branch. Its two denominators are attached to the
447: same two actual cap slots; a one denotes an absent or unconstraining
448: slot. The table is generated directly from the audited F53 catalogue.
449: 
450: | Branch | Source profile | (a,b) | Cap denominators | Minimum winding |
